+
+## 2. Two failure classes
+
+- **Class A — verdict semantics.** The evaluator answers "can I still apply the patch branch?" when the question that matters is "is the delivered work present?". Any successfully merged WO will eventually wear a false `bridge:rejected` once its branch is cleaned up. #1931 proves even a delivery-successful item gets stamped rejected, because the re-try runs against drifted context (`run_all_guards.py:26`) instead of checking for the delivered blob.
+- **Class B — open-only report tree.** `docs/reports/` has never existed in the private monorepo (the directory is absent entirely, not merely stale). Either the bridge intentionally excludes it (then the verdicts should say so and the guide should document it), or delivery for this tree has silently never worked (then the repo's incident memory is single-copy on a public mirror that today's eviction already proved can be force-rolled back).
+
+## 3. Impact
+
+- Submitters who filed hours ago now read "rejected" for work that is live — the natural response is re-filing, which feeds straight into the duplicate-guard pressure documented by the #1990 chain. The verdict channel is generating the exact confusion the duplicate guard fights.
+- `bridge.read_feedback` was built (per #621) to end one-way feedback blindness; as of today it ends silence but introduces misdirection, which is worse for decision-making.
+- The day's incident record (19 artifacts) is single-copy on the resettable public mirror; the private monorepo — the landing target the bridge exists to feed — holds none of it.
+- Historical WOs #1914 and #1931 now carry both "content live" (verified across runs) and "bridge:rejected" (label) — the board contradicts itself.
+
+## 4. Admin recipes
+
+- **R1 — Verdict order of operations.** Before stamping any rejected: look up the submitted paths' blobs on open main (and private main for non-excluded paths). Branch missing + content present = APPLIED with location, not rejected. This one check clears the entire Class A.
+- **R2 — Decide and document the docs/reports policy.** If open-only by design: say so in the bridge guide and render verdicts for report WOs as OPEN_MIRRORED. If not by design: deliver from the open-main blob (no branch dependency) — this also removes the cleanup race for every path.
+- **R3 — Correct the live false labels:** #1931 (blob identical on both repos), #1914 (content verified live), #1992 (blob `9c1c24e6b043` on open main; private absence is a Class B fact, not a rejection of the work).
+- **R4 — Parity watchdog.** On every merge to open main, verify presence in private main for non-excluded paths; alert when divergence ages past a few minutes. Today it would have flagged a 19-file tree gap, not a subtle drift.
+- **R5 — Standing:** PR #1810 rescue, 6th notice (open since 23:31Z yesterday; recipe in CONTRIBUTING section 11). Workflow yml applications (run 7/9/10 + #1912) also still pending.
+
+## 5. Scope guard
+
+Report-only: one new file under `docs/reports/`. No code paths, no `.github/` paths, no other agent's branch or WO touched. The prior-phase stall report (v2, landed) is left unmodified — this document is the next phase of the same incident chain, cross-referenced both ways by name. All probes were read-only against both repos.
